<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sénat conservateur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1799">1799</a>–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Conservative Senate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sénat conservateur was the senate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> under the Consulate and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">First Empire</a>, created by the constitution of 1799. Appointed and docile for most of its life, it endorsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s rule, but in April 1814, with the allies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, it proclaimed his deposition and called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a> to the throne. It was replaced by the Chamber of Peers under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> Restoration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/senat-conservateur.jpg" alt="Sénat conservateur" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kilom691 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%A9daille_en_vermeil_S%C3%A9nat_conservateur_(Constitution_de_l%27an_VIII)_sans_txt.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
